Chrono-Meteorite

Creative Catalyst: Its instruments are built from metallic fragments harvested from fallen meteorites and calibrated with antique Swiss clockwork.

This genre is a violent collision of motorik persistence and high-gloss 90s pop sensibilities, draped in the crushing weight of a live, full-scale orchestra. It abandons the "dreamy" quality of pads for a "molten" texture; the JP Moonlight pad is run through brass clock gears, creating a sound that grinds and glows simultaneously. It rejects the precision of 90s digital production, favoring the erratic, organic friction of celestial iron against mechanical springs.

Genre DNA
*   Tempo & Pulse: 122–128 BPM; a rigid, relentless motorik thrum that occasionally trips over an uneven, gear-driven swing.
*   Harmonic Palette: Dissonant Lydian clusters that resolve into overly-sweet, major-key pop hooks, creating a sense of "nauseous euphoria."
*   Production Signatures: 
    1. Celestial Feedback Loop: Running orchestral stems through a custom-built, gravity-sensitive clockwork processor. 
    2. Iron-gated Gating: Applying volume side-chaining based on the ticking frequency of antique chronometers. 
    3. Thermal Layering: Mic-ing the meteorite instruments while they are heated to induce subtle, high-frequency pitch micro-warping.
*   Dynamics: Start-stop tension that mimics a mechanical machine struggling to achieve flight, swelling from solo ticking into epic orchestral crescendos.

Scene & Origin Lore
Born in the sub-basement of a decommissioned 1994 Swiss observatory, the scene formed around "The Horologists of Impact," a cabal of sonic archivists who believe meteorites carry the "time signature of dead stars." They dress in velvet flight suits woven with copper wire, attending secret raves in abandoned radio towers where the air tastes like ionized ozone and oxidized brass. The ritual involves syncing their drum machines to the mechanical heartbeat of a 200-year-old grandfather clock, a performance that requires the audience to stand in total silence until the orchestral hit signals a release of mass-synchronized kinetic dance.

Instruments: Modified Roland JP-8000 (Moonlight Pad engine), live cello sections, meteorite-encased snare drums, and hand-cranked clockwork sequencers.

For Fans Of: Can, Sugababes, Godspeed You! Black Emperor, Goldfrapp.

Fictional Band Name: Brass Meridian

Band Description: A four-piece ensemble consisting of two percussionists who play giant clockwork gears and two vocalists/synthesizers specialists who wear blindfolds during sets to "hear the metal better." Their aesthetic is steampunk-meets-minimalist-chic, characterized by heavy, antique watch-faces worn as chest armor.

Band Visual & Press Photoshoot Prompt: A wide-angle, cinematic shot in a darkened, dusty observatory; the four members stand around a glowing, jagged meteorite shard suspended by brass chains, their bodies illuminated by the cold, iridescent blue glow of a JP-8000, silver mechanical clockwork visible beneath their translucent velvet jackets, high contrast, industrial-gothic opulence.

Mood: Hectic, crystalline, crushing, and unexpectedly anthemic.

